# Steep demo conventions

When the user asks for Steep metrics, Steep-as-code YAML, semantic modules, join paths, KPIs, or "add metrics for [team]":

0. Read `docs/cursor-steep-guidance.md` first when joins, cardinality, or cross-table metrics are involved — only `one-to-one` and `one-to-many` exist; declare paths from the correct `module.table` with real `from`/`to` column names (not SQL many-to-one intuition alone).
1. Read `business-context.md` first. Respect the sensitive-field deny list in section 3 when choosing `dimensions` on metrics. Use team section 2 for `category` and `owner_emails` when the user names a team or when generating that team's top questions.
2. Follow `.cursor/skills/generate-steep-modules/SKILL.md` end-to-end.
3. Use `star-schema/models/marts/schema.yml` under `meta.steep` for module identifiers, target `schema`/`table`, `default_time_column`, `join_paths`, and `reference_metrics`. Use per-column `meta.dimension_type` and `example_values` for dimensions and filters. **dbt `description` fields must stay data/business documentation** (no Steep product copy). Steep `dimensions[].description` should **verbatim-copy** that dbt column `description` whenever present; Steep-only explanations belong in `modules/*.yaml` comments sparingly or in Steep docs—not in `schema.yml` descriptions. Every generated metric must include a concise business `description` and a relevant non-empty `dimensions` list. For a **plain-dbt** run, the user may ask to ignore `meta.steep` and infer from SQL + descriptions.
4. For filters and slices, use only `example_values` from `schema.yml` (or literals the user gave in chat). Optional BigQuery MCP if the user asked to verify distinct values in the warehouse.
5. If the user asks to "restart", "reset", "delete modules", "delete all modules", or clear/rebuild the semantic layer, **do not delete every YAML file**. Leave exactly one module with exactly one metric so Steep can sync the cleanup; prefer a tiny `modules/customers.yaml` containing only `total_customers`.
6. Write output under `modules/` (create if missing), one YAML file per `module_identifier`, 2-space indentation, unique metric identifiers across all files. In git, **proactively** create and check out a feature branch (`git checkout -b …`) **before** adding or changing those files — do not wait for the user to ask; then push for Steep sync / PR (see SKILL Step 2b). Do not land module-only work straight on `main` when PR rules apply.
7. Validate against `.cursor/skills/generate-steep-modules/references/yaml-schema-reference.md`; if unsure, defer to Steep [Code Reference](https://help.steep.app/setup-and-manage/code-reference). Index of Help links: `docs/README.md`.

If the user explicitly wants a Markdown-only workflow, point them to `docs/STEEP-AS-CODE.md`.
